04 #edb/ref 17592186045746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematical Foundations I" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1008 "Mathematical Foundations I is the first course in a sequence specially crafted for adults seeking the most direct path to prepare for university math courses. The course starts with fraction arithmetic and covers all the way up through the algebra of linear equations. Upon completing the course, students will be prepared for Mathematical Foundations II, the second step of the journey towards university-level math." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1009 "Solidify your arithmetic, learn about variables and graphs, level up your algebra, and learn the essentials of geometry." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1012 113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1013 "MF1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046648 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "042e624c-a46d-509f-a48e-61bd6d3e082c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048987 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048987 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186142845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048987 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186142846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048987 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186142847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048987 #edb/ref 1031 "Dividing Whole Numbers by Fractions Using Models: Whole Number Results" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048987 #edb/ref 1033 2437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048987 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d12cbe5f-b948-5cf0-8d57-b3147ff8483b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049287 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049287 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049287 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049287 #edb/ref 1031 "Dividing Whole Numbers by Fractions Using Models: Fractional Results" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049287 #edb/ref 1033 4698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049287 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "ea012716-aa80-521b-a85c-3a4cdebc3bfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050842 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050842 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186142823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050842 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186142824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050842 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186142825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050842 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186142826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050842 #edb/ref 1031 "Dividing Fractions Using Models" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050842 #edb/ref 1033 2438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050842 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6ae717f0-85c1-5ce8-a9a5-f5bd8976a35e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203677 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203677 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n$0$\n\n![](images/e6/e60ebf4ac02326cff610f1d4238bb2b8c16062aaa8a50c7cc040631e65d3a5b0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203677 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0cecb07f-726d-5d91-8cef-a865aa0feecf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203677 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $2$ wholes split into sixths.![](images/6c/6c4328991ef5f89f91b3df5b4d43f8034d58249442700d59c2d842a44c9e8cda.png)\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}$ sixths in each group, with $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ remaining.![](images/e0/e08a28fee137c40615d7261b6282dce8dd0392084a8d801f9cc848364a3ad0f5.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- There are $\\color{red}2$ full groups.\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ sixths left over out of a possible group of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}$. So the fractional part of our answer is\n$\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{5}{6} = {\\color{red}{2}} \\, \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203679 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203679 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/60/60034ce6b9e7e7e5cd24c8f6f2f3d80a1bbc5d3a8532f3c4ccaeb3d89eaaeb1d.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate the value of $2 \\div \\dfrac{3}{7}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203679 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89b0b6f9-d6e3-5311-9002-6f6a09b60692" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203679 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\n, we first need to divide each of the $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}7$ equal parts.\n\n![](images/a3/a373e9e5c6a5dc23240029d2d89fef66f4b9959ffef349f5ea351fffc897d3c8.png)\n\nNext, we break this into groups of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}$:\n\n![](images/1c/1cfe8f5a08ee9464489cacef94b0d94982def065a9ab290acc9ebbec6efc7bf7.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We got $\\fbox{4}$ full groups.\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ pieces left over out of a possible group of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}$. So the fractional part of our answer is\n$\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{3}{7} = \\fbox{4} \\, \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203967 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203967 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203967 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203967 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Remainder in a Division Problem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203967 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203967 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d71689a0-2245-5bb4-97fb-47accd5ae8aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203968 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203968 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203968 #edb/ref 1039 "Dividing a Whole Number by a Non-Unit Fraction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203968 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203968 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "318e702c-5064-5160-aba1-98c52bc9a343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204108 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204108 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204108 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204108 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204108 #edb/ref 1045 "Dividing Whole Numbers by Fractions Using Models: Fractional Results" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204108 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186204789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204108 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204108 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5956f6cf-ca30-58d2-9ee1-6fa4e250cacf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204789 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204789 #edb/ref 1061 195193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204789 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fb2d5453-bf96-5349-9538-5fa183ce9885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204789 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204790 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204790 #edb/ref 1061 195194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204790 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5009403-d4d6-5b89-8c2e-15432efa8c66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204790 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204791 #edb/ref 1061 195197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204791 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "709abbf7-e660-58aa-b185-632b0c5fc053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204791 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205225 #edb/ref 1064 "When we divide a whole number by a non-unit fraction, the answer is often not a whole number.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to solve division problems in these cases using models. The process is similar to cases when the answer is a whole number (we saw this in a previous lesson). The difference now is that we have to deal with a remainder.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's use a model to solve the following division problem:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe start with a model that represents $\\color{blue}1$ whole:\n\n![](images/bf/bf3da76c75a78c8f2fd64f0ad08a7de339734de12a927d164e7b0b85be5c8bca.png)\n\nWe need to determine how many times\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}}3\n$$\n\nfits into $\\color{blue}1$ whole. So, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we split our whole into *thirds* (the denominator of our fraction):![](images/bf/bfebfebd8b72bbfe256f4cecadaa0b264be7640117b915aa574a485a0091ab09.png)\n- Next, we break this into groups of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}$:![](images/ac/ac3eaf757cee480d078152c974e98c9c9470cb0bb4f1ac36aefc973c40edfaef.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We have $\\fbox{1}$ full group. This is the whole number part of our answer.\n- We see that there is $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{1}$ piece left over out of a possible group of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}$. So, the fractional part of our answer is\n$\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{1}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}}$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our division problem is as follows:\n\n$$\n1 \\div \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\fbox{1} \\, \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{1}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205225 #edb/ref 1065 8812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205225 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ba2190d9-d8e4-5d70-902a-fb76e0985e5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205225 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
